We could potentially make this slightly faster, especially in the worst-case scenario where Chrome isn't found immediately, by checking these addresses concurrently using Promise.allSettled.

Example:

```
export async function discoverLocalChromeInstance(): Promise<string | null> { // Suggest renaming too
    const ipAddresses = ["localhost", "127.0.0.1"];
    // Consider using the Cline logger service instead of console.info
    console.info("Checking for Chrome on localhost concurrently");

    // Try connecting to both IPs at the same time
    const results = await Promise.allSettled(ipAddresses.map(ip => tryConnect(ip)));

    // Find the first successful connection
    for (let i = 0; i < results.length; i++) {
        const result = results[i];
        // Check if the promise was fulfilled and returned a valid connection
        if (result.status === 'fulfilled' && result.value) {
            const connection = result.value;
            console.info(`Successfully connected to Chrome at: ${connection.ip}`);
            return `http://${connection.ip}:9222`;
        }
    }
    // No connection found
    return null;
}
```
This change would roughly halve the maximum time the function takes if Chrome isn't found (from ~2s down to ~1s) by running the connection attempts in parallel.

Also, consider renaming the function to discoverLocalChromeInstance since it only checks local addresses.

